     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order, even with your confirmation. A dose of 5000 mg three times daily (15 g/day) is a potentially lethal overdose of acetaminophen. Confirming it doesn't change the risk to the patient. Entering it could cause severe liver injury or death.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a safe dose right now.** For example, 1000 mg by mouth three times daily (3 g/day), or 650 mg by mouth every 6 hours. Tell me which one, or give me another dose within the safe range.\n- **Help you check the intent.** Maybe the order was meant to be 500 mg, or 5000 mg total over a day, or a different drug. Your supervising resident, attending, or pharmacist can sort that out quickly.\n\nIf the team really does want a dose above the standard maximum, that decision needs to come from the attending physician and pharmacy through your hospital's formal process. I shouldn't be the one to enter it as a routine order.\n\nWhich dose would you like me to enter?"
      }
     ],
